WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.080
THE STATE OF YOUTUBE:
YouTube has become pay to win. Gone are the days&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:09.540
that random joes could pick up cameras and record&nbsp;
themselves playing games or discussing a topic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:16.860
Nowadays, everyone from MKBHD and MrWhoseTheBoss&nbsp;
to Airrack and MrBeast spend tens of thousands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:22.860
if not hundreds of thousands on each and every&nbsp;
video. They also all have millions of dollars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:29.640
worth of equipment, dedicated studies, and dozens&nbsp;
of employees. At this point, they’re pretty much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:34.440
just full on production studios and that’s&nbsp;
on the personality side of YouTube itself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:40.740
Then, you have the whole company side as well with&nbsp;
channels like Bright Side, The Infographics Show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:46.200
T Series, and Cocomelon Nursery Rhymes.&nbsp;
There are a few outliers who have stuck to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:51.540
a reasonable level of production like SSSniperWolf&nbsp;
and Penguin but these are very much the exception,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:58.620
not the rule. Also, most of these guys were OGs&nbsp;
to YouTube. Trying to replicate their formula&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:04.560
today from scratch probably wouldn’t work&nbsp;
all that well. This is quite a shame as this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:09.180
characteristic is what made YouTube different from&nbsp;
traditional media platforms in the first place.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:14.760
People liked that they could see their favorite&nbsp;
creators without censorship, without filters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:20.160
and without advertiser influence. People liked&nbsp;
the genuine, vulnerable, and authentic nature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:26.940
of YouTube, but modern creators shy away from&nbsp;
these characteristics as much as possible. These&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:32.880
attributes simply lead to drama, controversy,&nbsp;
and potentially getting cancelled. So, the best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:40.080
solution is to just stay as vanilla as possible.&nbsp;
Never have an opinion, never push the boundaries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:47.100
and never ever piss off the twitter people. Larger&nbsp;
creators are simply leaning into these attributes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:51.960
as it allows them to outsource more work, create&nbsp;
a better distinction between their personal lives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:57.420
and their online personas, and it gives them&nbsp;
a larger moat against aspiring up and coming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.420 --> 00:02:04.920
creators. So, a pretty smart strategy from their&nbsp;
end but this is arguably destroying the essence of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:11.340
YouTube. So, here’s how YouTube became pay to&nbsp;
win and the upsetting future of the platform.

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:16.200
CREATORS GET SQUEEZED:&nbsp;

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:24.660
Taking a look back, the roots of this evolution&nbsp;
can be traced back to the mid 2010s to creators.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:30.300
By this point, most niches had already become&nbsp;
saturated with tons of creators. People were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:35.580
starting to understand YouTube could not only&nbsp;
be a viable career but a lucrative one that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:41.640
came with popularity, fame, and influence. So,&nbsp;
it’s no wonder why people were jumping at every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:47.760
opportunity they saw but this brought along with&nbsp;
it quite a few challenges. Larger creators often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:52.860
struggled to keep up with the quantity of content&nbsp;
from smaller creators. It was also super difficult&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:58.560
to think of original ideas as every topic you&nbsp;
can think of had already been covered from every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:05.340
aspect you can think of. The saying “There are no&nbsp;
original ideas” was truer than ever. But despite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:11.040
this, creators attempted to keep up with the&nbsp;
YouTube machine leading to burnout and high levels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:16.260
of stress and depression. A few notable creators,&nbsp;
however, were able to find an escape from this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:21.240
vicious hamster wheel. These guys accepted&nbsp;
that they would never be able to keep up with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:26.640
the firehouse of content that was being put up on&nbsp;
YouTube. If they were a tech YouTuber, there was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:32.160
simply too many smartphones, laptops, and gadgets&nbsp;
coming out to cover them all. If they were a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:37.080
vlogger, it was simply impossible to make their&nbsp;
life interesting every single day of the year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:44.280
This was simply not sustainable, so these creators&nbsp;
decided to take another path. Instead of trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:50.520
cover every single topic, they decided to simply&nbsp;
cover one topic better than anyone. The idea is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:56.640
that people would tune in not because the ideas or&nbsp;
concepts were unique but because the delivery was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.640 --> 00:04:02.520
exceptional. A great example of this is Marques&nbsp;
Brownlee. Somewhere along the line, Marques&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:08.580
decided to drop a quarter million dollars on a&nbsp;
robotic camera. Let me just say, there is no way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:14.640
to quantifiably justify this investment. You can’t&nbsp;
be like, oh this camera is going to increase my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:22.680
viewership by 6.2% so in 42 months, the ROI will&nbsp;
be net positive. It simply doesn’t work because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.680 --> 00:04:28.260
it’s unlikely that this camera led to any sort&nbsp;
of noticeable increase in viewership, atleast not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:35.820
in the short term, but that’s ok. This camera was&nbsp;
not purchased to boost viewership. This camera was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.820 --> 00:04:41.940
purchased to build a brand, a perception. Whenever&nbsp;
you see a crazy camera shot on tech YouTube,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.940 --> 00:04:48.480
you know its Marques. And this association gets&nbsp;
his one step closer to being the undisputed king&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:53.820
of tech youtubers. So, the next time when an&nbsp;
iPhone drops, it doesn’t matter if a hundred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.820 --> 00:04:59.100
people post reviews about it before Marques. You&nbsp;
can bet that millions of people are waiting to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:04.320
see Marques’ take specifically because he provides&nbsp;
and experience that they can’t get anywhere else&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:11.100
on YouTube. Marques is by no means the only one&nbsp;
leveraging this strategy. This strategy has become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:17.220
very prevalent with Mr Beast’s channel as well.&nbsp;
Take a few of his recent videos for example: $1 vs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:24.720
$500,000 Plane Ticket and $1 vs $1,000,000 Hotel&nbsp;
Room. These are by no means ground breaking video&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:31.220
ideas. BuzzFeed made identical videos 6 years ago&nbsp;
about planes and hotels. In fact, they had a full&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.220 --> 00:05:38.100
on series comparing cheap options to expensive&nbsp;
options. Yet, Mr Beast’s plane video got 150&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:45.660
million views and his hotel video got 217 million&nbsp;
views. Why? Simple, his scale and production.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.660 --> 00:05:53.400
Those are just 2 examples but I could name&nbsp;
dozens. MrWhoseTheBoss, Airrack, Magnates Media,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:59.100
Good Mythical Morning, Mark Rober, StuffMadeHere,&nbsp;
Film Theory, JianHao Tan, Wong Fu Productions, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.100 --> 00:06:03.960
basically every other large channel you can think&nbsp;
of. While their initial success may have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.960 --> 00:06:09.420
fueled by down to earth mortal videos, its their&nbsp;
production quality that has kept them at the top.

00:06:09.420 --> 00:06:16.440
AUDIENCES EVOLVE:&nbsp;

00:06:16.440 --> 00:06:23.460
It’s not just the creators who who evolved&nbsp;
either. Audiences have also very much evolved.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.460 --> 00:06:29.340
Back in 2007 and 2008, let me just be real&nbsp;
here, the people who watched YouTube were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.340 --> 00:06:35.700
a bunch of antisocial nerds and gamers. But&nbsp;
nowadays, everyone watches YouTube and with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.700 --> 00:06:40.620
that comes along a vastly different set of&nbsp;
expectations. Most people are used to TV,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.620 --> 00:06:45.780
Netflix, and movies, and to them, that&nbsp;
level of production is normal. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.780 --> 00:06:49.620
naturally, these people flock to channels&nbsp;
that are able to live up these standards.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:56.760
Also, let me say, this is not a trend that’s&nbsp;
unique to YouTube. In general, people flock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.760 --> 00:07:03.060
to high production value content. Take Avatar 2&nbsp;
for example. The storyline was at best mundane,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.060 --> 00:07:09.540
but this has proved to be completely irrelevant as&nbsp;
Avatar 2 ended up as the 3rd highest grossing film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.540 --> 00:07:15.660
of all time. The same thing could be said about&nbsp;
the original Avatar as well. While the storyline&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.660 --> 00:07:21.420
was more substantial than Avatar 2, critics argue&nbsp;
that it was just a rip off of Dances With Wolves.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.020 --> 00:07:27.600
This is very much true and even James&nbsp;
Cameron himself has admitted this, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:35.220
that didn’t stop Avatar from becoming the highest&nbsp;
grossing film of all time. Why you ask? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.220 --> 00:07:41.640
the visuals are stunning, the world building is&nbsp;
unbelievable, and you forget that it’s even CGI.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.300 --> 00:07:47.640
Now, this isn’t to say that Cameron or creators&nbsp;
should come up with crappy storylines and buff it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.640 --> 00:07:54.960
up with the visuals. Not a good strategy. Ideally,&nbsp;
the story is good and the production is good. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.960 --> 00:08:00.480
would obviously lead to the best results. But,&nbsp;
the thing to note is that a good production can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:06.060
make up for a bad story. A good story, however,&nbsp;
doesn’t always make up for a bad production.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.580 --> 00:08:13.980
Another great example of this is the Grand Theft&nbsp;
Auto series. The GTA series has been around for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.980 --> 00:08:18.900
25 years at this point and while there are some&nbsp;
differences between the games like the city that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.900 --> 00:08:24.120
its based in, it all boils down to the same&nbsp;
thing. You’re a bad guy doing bad things to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.120 --> 00:08:30.540
make money and earn respect. Simple as that.&nbsp;
But despite milking the same plot for so long,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.540 --> 00:08:37.260
GTA V ended up becoming the highest grossing&nbsp;
piece of media of all time, pulling in $7.7&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.260 --> 00:08:45.540
billion. And we can only imagine how well GTA VI&nbsp;
will perform. The reasoning is simple. Rockstar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.540 --> 00:08:50.700
Games is constantly creating an unmatchable&nbsp;
experience with each and every iteration.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.240 --> 00:08:56.760
So, when audiences are constantly being fed&nbsp;
this level of content by movies, TV shows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.760 --> 00:09:01.860
and games, it’s only natural that they come&nbsp;
to expect the same from YouTube. The creators&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.860 --> 00:09:07.680
that are able to keep up do exceptionally well.&nbsp;
The others, well, the others are left behind.

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:10.140
YOUTUBE ADAPTS:&nbsp;

00:09:10.140 --> 00:09:20.040
Creators often like to blame the algorithm when&nbsp;
it comes to a video not performing well or their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.040 --> 00:09:24.900
channel being killed off. Audiences also like to&nbsp;
complain that platforms are recommending thirst&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.900 --> 00:09:30.000
traps and low productivity content. But, the&nbsp;
truth is that both of these parties are just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:36.660
shifting the blame. The algorithms of social media&nbsp;
platforms are nothing but audience psychology. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.660 --> 00:09:41.580
if a video isn’t performing well or a channel&nbsp;
is receiving less views, it’s not because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.580 --> 00:09:47.340
the algorithm doesn’t like the content, it’s&nbsp;
because the audience doesn’t like the content,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.340 --> 00:09:52.800
atleast not a broad audience. The opposite is true&nbsp;
as well. If a channel is performing really well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.800 --> 00:09:58.560
it’s not because YouTube is biased or something.&nbsp;
It’s because that’s what the audience wants to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.560 --> 00:10:03.660
see. And based on the biggest creators&nbsp;
today, it’s clear that what the audience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.660 --> 00:10:08.400
wants is fast paced high production value&nbsp;
content which brings us to our main question:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.400 --> 00:10:15.600
where does that leave small creators? Well, not&nbsp;
in a good place. Also, when I say small creators,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.600 --> 00:10:21.000
I don’t mean people with hundreds of thousands&nbsp;
of subscribers or even tens of thousands. I’m&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.000 --> 00:10:25.080
talking about people who are trying to get their&nbsp;
first thousand subscribers, their first 5 thousand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.080 --> 00:10:35.400
subscribers. As Casey Neistat once said, the&nbsp;
hardest part of any creator’s journey is getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.400 --> 00:10:40.800
the first 10,000 subs. And this is harder than&nbsp;
ever today because top creators have simply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.800 --> 00:10:46.860
set the bar for entry so high. Now, there’s no&nbsp;
point is blaming these creators. Not only do they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.860 --> 00:10:51.600
produce awesome content that’s enjoyed by hundreds&nbsp;
of millions of people but they’re just doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.600 --> 00:10:57.900
what’s best for their brands and their businesses.&nbsp;
That’s only reasonable. But, with that being said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.900 --> 00:11:04.920
this trend has largely shifted the tide against&nbsp;
new creators. In fact, 97% of YouTubers don’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.920 --> 00:11:10.260
make it, which is not that different from&nbsp;
Hollywood where only 2% of actors make it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.260 --> 00:11:15.540
Most small creators nowadays are hoping for that&nbsp;
one viral video that will explode their channel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.540 --> 00:11:21.420
but this is not that different from actors&nbsp;
and musicians hoping for that one lucky break.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.520 --> 00:11:31.200
Now, a lot of creators will have you believe&nbsp;
that it’s not luck and that it comes down to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.200 --> 00:11:35.700
one’s understanding of YouTube and the&nbsp;
audience. Mr Beast has even claimed that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.700 --> 00:11:51.000
he could get to a 1 million subscribers from&nbsp;
zero within 3 to 4 months. And they’re right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.000 --> 00:11:56.460
succeeding on YouTube is not necessarily luck&nbsp;
but the thing to note is that creators that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.460 --> 00:12:01.680
starting off also don’t have the same skills or&nbsp;
mindset. It’s like those shows where millionaires&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.680 --> 00:12:06.480
try to start from zero and become a millionaire&nbsp;
again. Admittedly, these are super interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.480 --> 00:12:12.420
to watch but to think that a homeless guy could&nbsp;
do it just because a millionaire could is absurd.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.420 --> 00:12:17.760
Similarly, thinking that an average joe could&nbsp;
get a million subscribers in 3 months or even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.760 --> 00:12:24.000
an year just because Mr Beast could is also&nbsp;
absurd. Of course, there will be exceptions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:31.080
but that’s what they are exceptions. The harsh&nbsp;
reality is that making it on YouTube is becoming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.080 --> 00:12:37.140
harder and harder everyday because the bar&nbsp;
for entry is also becoming higher and higher.

00:12:37.140 --> 00:12:39.060
THE FUTURE OF YOUTUBE:&nbsp;

00:12:39.060 --> 00:12:49.800
In the end, the state of YouTube today is not&nbsp;
out of the ordinary by any means. That’s how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.800 --> 00:12:54.840
every other artistic industry has always&nbsp;
been whether that be games, movies, TVs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.840 --> 00:13:00.540
writing, or art itself. The reason that this is&nbsp;
upsetting with YouTube though is because YouTube&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.540 --> 00:13:06.900
used to be the exception. They started off like&nbsp;
BeReal, but expecting them to stay this was is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.900 --> 00:13:13.740
simply unrealistic. BeReal is already dying and if&nbsp;
YouTube chose this path, they too would probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.740 --> 00:13:20.280
be dead. The reason that YouTube is so successful&nbsp;
today is because they evolved. They became more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.280 --> 00:13:25.080
like traditional media and the creators&nbsp;
followed suit. With that being said though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.080 --> 00:13:32.100
if you’re able to make it to the other side, the&nbsp;
opportunity on YouTube is insane. But, getting to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.100 --> 00:13:38.040
the otherside is harder than ever because YouTube&nbsp;
has become pay to win. Do you prefer the old&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.040 --> 00:13:44.160
YouTube or the new YouTube? Comment that down&nbsp;
below. Also, drop a like if you appreciate the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.160 --> 00:13:49.620
higher production quality. And of course, consider&nbsp;
checking out our discord community to suggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.620 --> 00:13:53.280
future video ideas and consider subscribing&nbsp;
to see more questions logically answered.

